  first half is `pderiv_eq` and the second is `isUnit_det`.

# SECOND FALSITY AUDIT (2026-07-28): the RISK FLAGGED against `pderiv_eq` is
DISCHARGED — the field is FAITHFUL as stated, and must NOT be weakened

THE FLAG, recorded here earlier the same day and left standing for the next
owner: `pderiv_eq` asserts `MvPowerSeries.pderiv j (P i) = 3 * Q i j` as an
identity in `𝒪₃ᵥ[[X₁,…,X_h]]`, whereas Fontaine's step 3 and the transcription
three bullets above claim only `(∂P_i/∂X_j)(x) ∈ 3A`, an identity in `A ⧸ J`.
The power-series form IS strictly stronger, and the natural proof really does
reach only `∂P_i/∂X_j ∈ 3·𝒪₃ᵥ[[X]] + ker α`.  The flag then GUESSED that
absorbing the `ker α` term means replacing `P_i` by `Σ_k u_ik P_k` and SOLVING
`∂u/∂X_j = −u·C_j`, a first-order system over `𝔽₃[[X]]` with an integrability
condition and, worse, with `∂` in characteristic `3` having an enormous
cokernel.  **That guess is wrong: no differential equation has to be solved.**
This is a re-audit against the CURRENT composite statement — i.e. including the
field `idempotent_generator` added earlier today; the earlier audit of
`separation`/`mem_of_algHom` is VOID for this purpose and is not inherited.

NOTATION for the argument.  `B := A ⧸ J`; `R := 𝒪₃ᵥ[[X₁,…,X_h]]`; `I := ker α`;
`R̄ := R/3R = 𝔽₃[[X]]` — legitimate because `𝒪₃ᵥ` is the `3`-adic completion of
`ℤ`, hence UNRAMIFIED, so `3` is a uniformiser and `𝒪₃ᵥ/3 = 𝔽₃`;
`Ī := (I + 3R)/3R`; `p_i := P_i mod 3`; `B̄ := B/3B`.

1. `J = (ε)` with `ε` idempotent (`idempotent_generator`), so `A ≅ B × A/(1−ε)`
   and BOTH `hΩ` and the flatness half of `hfon` pass to the factor `B`.  And
   `Ω[B⁄𝒪₃ᵥ] = B^h/Jac(P)` honestly, with no continuous-differentials subtlety
   even though `Ω_{R⁄𝒪₃ᵥ}` is not finitely generated: `B` is FINITE over `𝒪₃ᵥ`,
   so the finite submodule `𝒪₃ᵥ[x₁,…,x_h]` is complete, hence closed, hence all
   of `B`, i.e. `B` is generated as an `𝒪₃ᵥ`-ALGEBRA by the coordinates.
2. Minimality of the presentation puts every Jacobian entry in `𝔪_B`, so
   `Ω ⊗_B κ ≅ κ^h`; `hfon`'s flatness half then makes `Ω` FREE over the artinian
   local ring `B̄`, necessarily of rank `h`.  `hΩ` gives `3·B^h ⊆ Jac`, so
   `Ω = B̄^h/J̄ac ≅ B̄^h`, and a length count over the finite-length `B̄` forces
   `J̄ac = 0`.  So `∂p_i/∂X_j ∈ Ī` for all `i, j` — and hence, since
   `∂(Σ a_i p_i) = Σ (∂a_i) p_i + Σ a_i (∂p_i)`, **`Ī` is a DIFFERENTIAL IDEAL**:
   stable under every `∂/∂X_j`.  This is the step the flag missed.
3. KEY LEMMA (`k` any field of characteristic `p`, `R̄ = k[[X₁,…,X_h]]`,
   `S := k[[X₁^p,…,X_h^p]]`): an ideal `𝔞 ⊆ R̄` stable under all `∂/∂X_j`
   satisfies `𝔞 = (𝔞 ∩ S)·R̄`.  PROOF, three lines and no PDE: `R̄` is free over
   `S` on the box basis `{X^a : 0 ≤ a_j < p}` and each `∂_j` is `S`-linear, so
   for `b` in the box the Hasse derivative `D^{(b)} = ∏_j ∂_j^{b_j}/b_j!` — the
   division is LEGITIMATE because `b_j < p` makes `b_j!` invertible — also
   preserves `𝔞`.  Write `f = Σ_a s_a X^a ∈ 𝔞` with `s_a ∈ S`, take `a₀`
   MAXIMAL in the (finite) support for the componentwise order and apply
   `D^{(a₀)}`: no `a > a₀` survives in the support, `binom(a_j, a₀_j) ≠ 0` in `k`
   for `a_j, a₀_j < p`, and `D^{(a₀)}` is `S`-linear, so
   `D^{(a₀)} f = s_{a₀} ∈ 𝔞 ∩ S`.  Subtract and induct on the support.  Over the
   perfect field `𝔽₃`, `S` is exactly the set of CUBES
   (`(Σ c_a X^a)³ = Σ c_a X^{3a}`), and every element of `S` has ALL partial
   derivatives ZERO.
4. So `Ī` has a generating set inside `S`, and it needs exactly `h` generators:
   `ht Ī = dim R̄ − dim B̄ = h`, so Krull's height theorem bounds `μ(Ī) ≥ h`,
   while the `p_i` bound it above.  Choose `q₁,…,q_h ∈ Ī ∩ S` mapping to a basis
   of `Ī/𝔫̄Ī` and lift each to some `P'_i ∈ I`.  Then `I = (P'₁,…,P'_h) + 3I` —
   for `f ∈ I` write `f − Σ a_i P'_i = 3g`, which lies in `I`, and `3g ∈ I`
   implies `g ∈ I` because `B = R/I` is `3`-torsion-free (`A` is `𝒪₃ᵥ`-flat) —
   and Nakayama over the local ring `R` upgrades that to `I = (P'₁,…,P'_h)`.
5. By construction `P'_i mod 3 = q_i ∈ S`, i.e. `3 ∣ ∂P'_i/∂X_j` in `R` for
   every `i, j`.  `R` is `3`-torsion-free, so `Q i j := (∂P'_i/∂X_j)/3` is well
   defined and `pderiv_eq` holds ON THE NOSE, exactly as the field states it.
6. `isUnit_det` comes free from the same input: `Ω = B^h/Jac(P') = B^h/3·α(Q)B^h`
   is free of rank `h` over `B̄`, which forces `3·α(Q)B^h = 3B^h`; `3` is a
   nonzerodivisor on `B`, so `α(Q)` is a SURJECTIVE endomorphism of the finite
   module `B^h`, hence an isomorphism, hence `det α(Q)` is a unit.

CONSEQUENCE FOR THE STATEMENT: **nothing changes.**  The proposed weakening to
`α (MvPowerSeries.pderiv j (P i)) = 3 * α (Q i j)` is NOT needed and must not be
applied.  To be exact about what it would cost — the weak form would in fact
still carry both consumers, since `taylor_of_isFontainePresentation`'s Leibniz
induction closes modulo `ker α` either way — so this is not a correctness
argument but a cost one: the strong form is TRUE, it is what Fontaine's own
choice of generators satisfies, and it turns that induction into the identity
`∂(f·Pᵢ)/∂X_j = (∂f/∂X_j)·Pᵢ + f·3·Q_ij` in `𝒪₃ᵥ[[X]]` instead of a congruence
that has to be tracked through every step.  Weakening a true field to make a
sorried leaf look easier is the wrong trade.

SANITY CHECKS ACTUALLY RUN.
(a) `μ₃`: `A = ℤ₃[X]/(X³+3X²+3X)`.  `P ≡ X³` is a cube, `∂P/∂X = 3(X+1)²`, so
`Q = (X+1)²` and `α Q` is a unit — the strong form holds verbatim, with no
change of generator.
(b) The obstruction the KEY LEMMA removes is REAL for non-Fontaine algebras,
which is why the flag looked plausible: `A = ℤ₃[√3] = ℤ₃[X]/(X²−3)` has
`∂P/∂X = 2X`, and no rescaling produces a factor of `3` (mod `3`,
`(uP)' = u'X² + 2uX = 0` forces `u(0) = 0`, so `u` is not a unit).  It is
excluded by `hfon`, NOT by `hΩ`: `Ω = 𝔽₃` has length `1` over
`B̄ = 𝔽₃[X]/(X²)` of length `2`, so `Ω` is not free, i.e. not flat.  Note `hΩ`
alone would have admitted it — the flatness half of `hfon` is load-bearing here
and cannot be dropped.
(c) The `h = 1` case of the whole argument in closed form: a Weierstrass
generator of degree `n` has `P ≡ X^n mod 3`, and "`Ω` free of rank `1` over `B̄`"
says `(P, P') = (P, 3)`, which mod `3` reads `nX^{n−1} ∈ (X^n)` and therefore
forces `3 ∣ n`; but then `P' ≡ nX^{n−1} ≡ 0 mod 3` already, so `3 ∣ P'` with no
change of generator at all.  (And `ℤ₃[3^{1/3}] = ℤ₃[X]/(X³−3)`, the obvious
`3 ∣ n` candidate for a counterexample, is killed by `hΩ` instead:
`Ω = ℤ₃³/⟨9, 9X, 3X²⟩ ≅ ℤ/9 ⊕ ℤ/9 ⊕ ℤ/3` is not killed by `3`.)

THE DECOMPOSITION BELOW follows the audit exactly: step 4's hypothesis is what
`exists_minimalIdempotentPresentation` (steps 1–2) has to deliver, and items
2–6 are `exists_pderiv_eq_of_minimalPresentation` (step 3). -/
theorem exists_fontainePresentation
    (A : Type) [CommRing A] [Algebra 𝒪₃ᵥ A] [Module.Flat 𝒪₃ᵥ A]
    [Module.Finite 𝒪₃ᵥ A]
    (hΩ : ∀ ω : Ω[A⁄𝒪₃ᵥ], (3 : ℕ) • ω = 0)
    (hfon : IsFontaineAlgebra A)
    (E : IntermediateField ℚ₃ᵥ ℚ₃ᵥᵃˡᵍ) [FiniteDimensional ℚ₃ᵥ E]
    (ū : A →ₐ[𝒪₃ᵥ] (IntegralClosure 𝒪₃ᵥ E ⧸
      IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal (IntegralClosure 𝒪₃ᵥ E) ^ 1)) :
    ∃ (h : ℕ) (x : Fin h → A) (J : Ideal A)
      (P : Fin h → MvPowerSeries (Fin h) 𝒪₃ᵥ)
      (Q : Fin h → Fin h → MvPowerSeries (Fin h) 𝒪₃ᵥ)
      (α : MvPowerSeries (Fin h) 𝒪₃ᵥ →ₐ[𝒪₃ᵥ] A ⧸ J),
      IsFontainePresentation A E ū h x J P Q α := by
  obtain ⟨h, x, J, P, α, hJ0, hsurj, hX, hker, hres, hidem, hloc, hmin⟩ :=
    exists_minimalIdempotentPresentation A hfon E ū
  obtain ⟨P', Q, hker', hpd, hdet⟩ :=
    exists_pderiv_eq_of_minimalPresentation A hΩ hfon h J P α hidem hloc hsurj hker hmin
  exact ⟨h, x, J, P', Q, α,
    { map_eq_zero := hJ0
      surjective := hsurj
      map_X := hX
      ker_eq := hker'
      residue_eq_zero := hres
      pderiv_eq := hpd
      isUnit_det := hdet
      idempotent_generator := hidem }⟩

