(reported 2026-07-27, written down here 2026-07-28 — the note was deferred only
because this file costs ~84 minutes to rebuild).

`hmm₀ram` excludes an `mm₀` that MISSES a ramified prime. It says nothing when
there are no ramified primes to miss: for a `χ` unramified at every finite place
the hypothesis is **vacuous**, so `mm₀ = ⊤` is admissible input, and then the
support clause forces the output too —

* no height-one prime divides `⊤`, so `∀ w, w.asIdeal ∣ mm → w.asIdeal ∣ mm₀`
  says `mm` has no prime divisor at all; with `mm ≠ ⊥` that gives `mm = ⊤`;
* `Im = ⊤`, the membership clause being vacuous for the same reason;
* `P` is the group of NARROW PRINCIPAL divisors (`δ - 1 ∈ ⊤` is vacuous, so the
  congruence drops out and only total positivity survives);
* `N` is generated by `single v (orderOf (χ (globalFrob v)))` over **every** `v`.

**The leaf stays TRUE there** — it reads `#φ(Im) ≤ [Cl⁺(F) : im N]`, and `Cl⁺(F)`
is finite, so the right-hand side is a genuine finite index; `M/F` is then
contained in the narrow Hilbert class field and the Artin map `Cl⁺(F) ↠ Gal(M/F)`
kills the classes `[v] ^ f_v` generating `im N`, whence the index is at least
`#Gal(M/F) = #φ(Im)`. So this is a branch to PROVE, not to exclude.

*And it is not vacuous.* A nontrivial everywhere-unramified `χ` exists as soon as
`Cl⁺(F) ≠ 1`: take `F = ℚ(√-5)`, which is totally complex with `h = h⁺ = 2`
(PARI/GP, 2026-07-28), and `χ` the quadratic character cutting out its Hilbert
class field `F(i)`. Then `mm₀ = ⊤` satisfies every hypothesis, `mm = ⊤` is forced,
and the conclusion is the true and nontrivial `2 ≤ 2`. Any `ℓ` with `ℓ ≠ 3` and
`2 ∣ ℓ^k` works, e.g. `ℓ = 2`, `k = 1`, exactly as in the `ℚ(i)` audits.

**But any proof that assumes some prime divides `mm` is WRONG on this branch**, and
the `ℚ(i)` counterexamples above do not warn you about it: both have `χ` ramified
at `2`, so both live entirely inside the `mm ≠ ⊤` case. A prover reaching for "let
`v` be a prime dividing `mm`" — e.g. to build the conductor, to run an induction on
`supp mm`, or to invoke local behaviour at a ramified place — must first dispose of
the everywhere-unramified case separately. It is not a degenerate corner: it is the
whole unramified theory (see the cross-reference below), and it is where the
narrowness of `P` is doing the work.

